WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.199
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:05.840
ANU SRIVASTAVA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.750
My name is Anu
Srivastava, and I'm

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.290
excited to talk to you today
about the new Cloud Document AI

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.220
platform.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.650
Let's dive in to learn more.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.340
Document AI is Google's solution
for understanding documents

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.770
in what we consider
dark formats,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.850
such as PDFs, handwritten
forms, and images.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.640
Using the power
of AI, Document AI

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.620
turns these
unstructured documents

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.509
into structured,
actionable data.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.950
For example, you could
process multipage handwritten

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.350
applications with a form parser.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.830
There are also several
specialized parsers already

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.280
created, so you could be
batch processing tax documents

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.900
by this afternoon.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.350
With structured data, you can
fuel better business decisions,

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.670
ensure that your data is
accurate and compliant,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.810
and use your data to meet
customer expectations.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.820
Or hey, maybe
you're just sitting

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.820
on a pile of crumpled
receipts you want to digitize.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.820
It's easy to get started
with our specialized parsers,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.850
or bring your own document type
and test out our form parser.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.560
It's all in the new
Document AI platform.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.430
Let's take a look.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.970
The platform, available in
the Google Cloud Console,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:23.230
is where you can create, modify,
and test out your processor.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.300
We'll walk through an
example on the platform

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.790
and talk about what's
going on under the hood

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:31.240
to show you what makes
Document AI special.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.350
Today, we'll demo how to parse
a driver's license application

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.710
and show you how to integrate
it into your app or service.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.870
Here is an image on my form.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:44.380
You can see it's been damaged,
it's crumpled, and just

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.080
doesn't have great
image quality.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.630
I want to test out what the
generic form parser will

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.110
be able to extract, so I will
create an instance of it here.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.900
I intend to use it in my
application's code on more

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.620
documents, so these variables
up here will be important later.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:06.550
Let's test it out.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.910
You can see the key-value
pairs extracted.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.150
They're now in a digital
format, easy to use.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.470
So what happens under the
hood when you run a document

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:21.490
through a processor?

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.320
First, Doc Prep scans,
cleans, and extracts text

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.960
from the image using
Google OCR, which

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.410
supports over 150 languages.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.050
Next, the words, text,
and visual elements

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.955
are used for classification
when required.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.550
Google uses an ensemble
of natural-language models

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.480
to extract simple and
compound entities.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.230
When the entities are related,
they're grouped together.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.140
Last but not least,
your document

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.990
is packaged up in a
unified document format

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.700
and sent back to you.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.080
Now that we understand
what's going on,

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.430
let's integrate my processor
into my application.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.590
I'm showing some
Python code today,

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.850
but we have client libraries
available in several languages.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:21.700
This API is incredibly
simple for how much

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.100
is happening behind the scenes.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.780
All I need is the
name of my processor,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.770
which I can construct with the
variables from the Console,

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:33.970
and then just the bytes of
the document or the image.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:34.960
Voila.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.770
Now I can expect all the
spatial and text data back

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.890
in an easy-to-use JSON format.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.560
Also, with all of
these key-value pairs,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.340
it's easy to integrate this
data in other services, storage,

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.850
or visualization tools
I might be using.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:53.710
We're excited to
see what you can do

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.570
with the power of Document AI.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.280
Get started today,
and let us know

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.900
what use cases you're solving.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

